This repository was produced as coursework for the University of Haifa
"Orchestration of AI Agents" course (AY26), final project. It is shared
publicly for educational and evaluative purposes only (course grading,
peer review, and use as a learning reference by future students of the
same or similar courses).

Permission is granted to any person obtaining a copy of this repository
to view, run, and study its contents, and to reuse or adapt parts of it
for their own non-commercial, educational coursework, subject to the
following conditions:

1. This repository, or any submission derived from it, must not be
   presented as a verbatim submission template — per the course's own
   rules, it is a learning artifact, not a shortcut around building an
   independent solution.
2. Any reuse must retain attribution to this repository as the source
   of the reused portion.
3. This repository is provided "as is", without warranty of any kind,
   express or implied. It has not been reviewed for production use, and
   no part of it should be relied upon outside an educational context.

This license deliberately mirrors the "educational use license" terms
referenced by the course's own example repository (`docs/tasks.md`,
Appendix D, Sec. 15.5.1): reuse and adaptation for coursework is
welcomed, but the code is a starting point, not a substitute for the
authors' own solution against the full specification.

Third-party acknowledgements:
- The distributed peer-to-peer match protocol, board/scent/belief-map
  mechanics, and reliability layer described in this repository follow
  the course's own rulebook (`docs/tasks.md`) and Mandatory Parameters
  Table, which take precedence wherever this repository's design
  choices might diverge from them.
- The Gmail OAuth transport pattern (`src/police_thief/services/gmail_oauth.py`)
  was adapted from a proven pattern used in the authors' own prior,
  separate project for the same course track.
- The GUI's shared-board, click-to-move interaction model was inspired
  by (but not copied from) a course reference example repository
  (see `README.md` for the link), consistent with the reuse terms above.
